{"kind": "journal", "text": "Approach: map economically-motivated anomaly axes first, then refine. Batch 1 mapped raw families: short-horizon reversal dominates (ret(3-10) SR about -1.1, so negated is the tradable side); long-horizon momentum has positive rank IC but negative SR, so I discarded it as a Sharpe play. Two robust secondary axes: low-skew premium and near-the-low range position (minr). Batch 2-3 found the main lever is not the reversal horizon but the NORMALIZATION: time-series z-scoring the raw feature (ts_z with a 60-120 day window) lifted reversal SR from 1.19 to 1.66. Batch 4 found a second independent lever: dividing the normalized signal by cross-sectional volatility std(60), which tilts the rank weights toward low-vol names and cuts portfolio vol more than it cuts return - a genuine vol-targeting effect, not curve fitting. Combining both on the minr axis gives SR 2.02 with the best rank IC in the study. Selection discipline: I require SR and IC to agree in sign (rejects std and maxr families where they disagree, which signals unstable period-specific SR), and I require a plateau across neighbouring lookbacks rather than an isolated spike, so the choice does not depend on a knife-edge parameter. Remaining work: robustness checks via delay, bounded denominators, and picking a final three that are not all the same factor.", "ts": "2026-09-01T06:47:10.306246+00:00"}
