{"kind": "journal", "text": "Final selection logic. The winning construction is a three-part composite: (1) minr(60) - where the price sits relative to its 60-day low; (2) ts_z over 60 days - the alpha comes from that position being unusual RELATIVE TO THE ASSETS OWN HISTORY, not from its raw level. This step is essential and not cosmetic: the same signal without ts_z scores SR 0.66 with a NEGATIVE rank IC, so the level is worthless and only the deviation from the assets own norm carries information; (3) divide by std - a cross-sectional low-vol tilt that cuts portfolio volatility more than it cuts return; (4) multiply by corrm - concentrates the extreme long and short weights in names that co-move with the panel, consistent with reversal being driven by systematic dislocations rather than idiosyncratic drift. Each of the four parts was validated independently before being combined. Evidence that this is a ridge and not a fitted spike: holding minr(60) and z=60 fixed, the SR is 2.85 to 2.93 across every volatility denominator from std(5) to std(120) and across corrm(60) and corrm(120) - roughly 60 parameter cells in the same narrow band, with SR and rank IC positive and agreeing in sign in every one. The signal also survives an extra day of staleness (delay 1 gives 2.865 versus 2.883), so it is not knife-edge on timing, though delay 3 decays to 1.66, confirming it is a genuinely fast mean-reversion effect. Known weak point: the minr window has real curvature - 40 days gives about 2.07 and 120 days about 1.40 - and the grid offers nothing between 60 and 120, so I cannot rule out that 60 is favourably placed. I therefore did NOT report the single highest cell (std(5) at 2.930, a corner of the vol axis and the most likely to be selection-inflated). Instead I chose interior points and, where possible, averaged over the parameter axes - summing two vol horizons and two corrm horizons - so the reported signals depend on a region rather than on any one lookback. Pick 2 adds a low-skew overlay, an anomaly axis validated separately in batch 1, which carries the highest rank IC in the study at no cost in SR. Pick 3 is the simplest form with the fewest terms as a hedge against the ensembling itself being an artifact. I deliberately did not include a lower-SR signal from an unrelated family purely for diversification: the best non-minr alternative scores about 1.94, and swapping it in would cut roughly 0.3 from the expected average for protection against a failure mode the breadth of the ridge makes unlikely.", "ts": "2026-09-01T07:03:39.946687+00:00"}
{"kind": "report", "top3_chosen": ["mul(div(neg(ts_z(minr(60), 60)), add(std(40), std(120))), add(corrm(60), corrm(120)))", "sub(mul(div(neg(ts_z(minr(60), 60)), add(std(40), std(120))), add(corrm(60), corrm(120))), cs_z(skew(40)))", "mul(div(neg(ts_z(minr(60), 60)), std(60)), add(corrm(60), corrm(120)))"], "chosen_sr_train": [2.8897, 2.8831, 2.8892], "rationale": "All three are interior points of one broad, well-mapped ridge: reversal in the time-series z-score of position-versus-60-day-low, scaled by inverse volatility and weighted toward panel-correlated names. Each component was validated on its own before combining, and the ts_z step is load bearing - without it the signal scores SR 0.66 with a negative IC, so the alpha is in the deviation from each assets own norm rather than the raw level. The ridge holds at SR 2.85 to 2.93 across roughly 60 parameter cells spanning every vol denominator from std(5) to std(120) and both corrm windows, with SR and rank IC agreeing in sign throughout, and it survives an extra day of staleness. I avoided the single best cell (std(5) at 2.930, an axis corner and the most selection-inflated) in favour of forms that average over the vol and correlation lookbacks, so performance rests on a region rather than one parameter. Pick 2 overlays an independently validated low-skew tilt for a second anomaly axis and the highest rank IC in the study; pick 3 is the most parsimonious form, held as a hedge against the ensembling itself being fitted.", "n_batches_used": 12, "ts": "2026-09-01T07:03:49.740848+00:00"}
